# Line endings are normalised to LF everywhere.
#
# This is not a style preference. The LICENSE checksum guard compares a sha256 taken from
# the published Apache text; if a checkout on Windows rewrote the file to CRLF, the same
# file would hash differently on a developer machine and in CI, and the guard would be
# reporting the platform rather than the licence.
#
# The same reasoning covers shell scripts and Gradle wrapper scripts, which must be LF to
# run at all on a Linux runner.

* text=auto eol=lf

LICENSE text eol=lf
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf

gradlew.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary.
*.jar binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary

# Measurement output is evidence, and a diff of it should be readable. These are text and
# are deliberately not marked binary -- a report that cites a number must be able to show
# the file the number came from changing.
*.json text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
